  "ownership_scope": "Product-owned dispatch_scheduling retains all twelve scheduling, delivery-attempt, lease, restart and migration cases. Both shared dispatch/request-construction authority oracles move unchanged apart from qualified owner imports and formatting. A direct product memory target executes the original two memory entries over those same oracles.",
  "compatibility_scope": "The legacy scheduling target aliases the released HIR/VM crates and includes the canonical product body. All twenty-three existing ephemeral-profile test bodies remain byte-identical; only the two oracle modules now include product-owned sources through local facade aliases. The distinct facade/product in-flight type-identity comparison remains unchanged.",
  "verification_scope": "Local macOS only. Baseline and legacy compatibility each execute the same thirty-five named cases. Direct product acceptance executes the corresponding twelve scheduling plus two memory cases; these are not fourteen newly invented scenarios or full replacement of the remaining facade tests. No-SQLite runs pass two product authority cases and twenty-three legacy memory cases; the complete product memory suite passes 169 tests. CI definitions and commands were checked locally, not run on a remote worker.",
  "export_scope": "The closed language test-support inventory requires only native_checkpoint.rs and native_group_checkpoint.rs. Product oracle files no longer exist in that directory and are not export prerequisites. Unknown files, directories, oversized files and native source symlinks remain rejected. Export contents stay byte-identical and temporary build targets are removed.",
  "remaining_boundaries": [
    "Complete declared-language and reference compatibility acceptance.",
    "Independent host-neutral production durability, real GUI/FFI integrations and physical repository extraction."
  ],
